WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.720
I'm in Toronto, Canada.

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:04.420
And Toronto is cold
for a lot of the year.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.490
Good winter boots are a necessity.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.250
They've got to be warm,
they've got to be waterproof,

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.393
but also, they've got to
stop you going arse over apex

00:00:11.393 --> 00:00:13.890
when you suddenly walk on a patch of ice.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.460
This is WinterLab...!

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.340
and it's part of the Challenging Environment
Assessment Laboratories

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.390
at the Toronto Rehabilitation Institute.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.833
And this is their tilting, frozen floor.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:29.457
- If you want to find out
whether people are at risk,

00:00:29.457 --> 00:00:33.110
at risk of falling,
at risk of a road accident,

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.420
at risk of something, you need
to put them in a challenging environment

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.620
and see how they respond.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.200
What we have is four modules
for different things.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.750
We have the world's most
realistic driving simulator now.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.590
When it rains in our
simulator, it actually rains.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.660
We also have StairLab, where we have
people walk up and down stairs,

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.040
and we suddenly and unexpectedly
move the room

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.500
and they have to grab the handrails

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.050
and we can see how effective the
handrails are, or the stair design.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:07.140
21,000 people in Ontario every winter,
on average, end up in

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.780
our emergency rooms because
they fall on the ice and snow.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:15.040
So the purpose of WinterLab is
so that we can do two things.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.980
One is that we can test the boots,
and inform consumers what they should buy

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.173
and the other is we can help companies
and ourselves develop better boots.

00:01:24.173 --> 00:01:27.388
- I know I'm harnessed in,
but that is terrifying!

00:01:27.388 --> 00:01:30.145
- It's an icebox really, it's insulated.

00:01:30.145 --> 00:01:33.900
We can generate winds, we can actually
make nice, white, fluffy snow,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.280
we can control the air temperature
very precisely

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.850
and we can control the temperature
of the ice very precisely.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:45.011
We actually have a group of...
graduate students(!)

00:01:45.011 --> 00:01:48.073
like every other university lab
you've ever been to,

00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:50.910
because they're not very expensive and
they're sort of disposable(!)

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:55.870
who do this testing and
because we want some uniformity.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.250
- It's a good harness, very good harness.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.887
- Now, in order to test
the performance of shoes,

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:07.083
we choose to put WinterLab on top
of a platform that we can gradually tip.

00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:09.600
- It does feel ever so slightly
like gravity is shifting.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.835
- We tip it as you walk up and down
until the moment that you slip.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:19.200
We have an angle which we
think is just acceptable.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.680
It's seven degrees.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.300
We actually take the minimum value
of several people

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.140
if you can make seven degrees up and down comfortably,

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.810
we give you one snowflake.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.367
- This is the good shoes,
I can just, oh, okay, yeah.

00:02:34.367 --> 00:02:35.970
No, I can literally just walk normally.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.400
- At the moment, we've got
a scale of three snowflakes

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.330
and that was because the
first time we ever did this,

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:47.030
90 out of 100 boots failed
to get one snowflake

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:48.790
and nothing got better than one snowflake

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:53.750
so to publish it as a five-snowflake scale
looked a bit depressing.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.835
We've already tested some
prototypes for next year

00:02:56.835 --> 00:03:00.204
and they're getting two
and three snowflakes.

00:03:00.204 --> 00:03:03.190
- That's fine, I put my foot down
and it just stays where it is.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.750
- We are really quite
confident that eventually

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:11.270
we're going to be able to add some more
snowflakes at the four and five level.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.260
It raises another interesting point, and that is:

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.080
can you have too much friction?

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.176
We're a long way off that yet,
but of course, more than half

00:03:20.176 --> 00:03:23.410
the falls that you experience
are people tripping up.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:29.550
We will eventually decide whether a
five-snowflake shoe is a good idea or not.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.410
I'm really happy, in two years
we've done two things.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.430
One is that we've changed
the buying habits of people

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.980
and the other is now the industry
is improving the quality of boots

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.760
and we're helping them do that.

00:03:41.771 --> 00:03:43.990
- Thank you very much to
all the team at WinterLab

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:47.047
here at the Toronto
Rehabilitation Institute.

00:03:47.047 --> 00:03:48.750
Pull down the links in the description

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.753
for more about them and their research.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.232
I swear I'm not deliberately
trying to do that.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:03:59.223
Yep!

